00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.690
book

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:50.850
We have about fifteen to twenty students in the course,
we're going to talk about that, 

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:51.540
we've talked a little bit about the

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.060
seminar and the field placement aspects already.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.479
Today we're going to have a very selective introduction.
This is an unusual course I think it probably

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:02.070
was the first one in the country on Autism,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.619
and still is one of the few of its type.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:07.570
I'm going to talk a little about diagnostic concepts,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.039
history, epidemiology, neurobiology

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:13.609
Dr. Klin's going to talk about social development,
psychological functioning

00:02:13.609 --> 00:02:19.159
and about the Autism program here at Yale, Then we'll have
some time for questions.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:23.829
Now, if you go on the internet if you
go to Wikipedia, which by the way, don't ever cite

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:25.299
in a paper,

00:02:25.299 --> 00:02:29.069
at least when you give it to me, but it's a handy source
of information. If you go to Wikipedia,

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:33.819
you will find a PDF file of the first paper
on Autism by a man named Leo Kanner.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:37.409
Leo Kanner was the first child psychiatrist
in the country he wrote a paper

00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:38.409
in 1943.

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:43.479
We're going to talk a lot more about that, and we'll have a chance to talk about
Leo Kanner several times during the course.

00:02:43.479 --> 00:02:47.630
One question is, did Autism exist before
Leo Kanner? Well, nobody recognized it.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.229
Certainly it must have existed. And in fact,

00:02:50.229 --> 00:02:53.040
if you go back historically this is a picture
of Victor

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.619
the wild boy in France

00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:58.430
probably many so-called feral children,
children

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.129
people thought were living with the animals,

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:03.839
probably were children who had Autism
and who had been abandoned.

00:03:03.839 --> 00:03:07.159
If you go back to read some of the reports of
these so-called feral children,

00:03:07.159 --> 00:03:11.499
they had many of the characteristics we see in children
with autism today.

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:15.569
In the 1860's, a Great British
psychiatrist, a man named Maudsley,

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:19.339
for the first time talked about children having
what he called "insanity".

00:03:19.339 --> 00:03:23.539
At that point in time, people who had no knowledge
of the kinds of things we talk about in terms of mental

00:03:23.539 --> 00:03:24.479
disorders today,

00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:26.609
they talked about things like insanity.

00:03:26.609 --> 00:03:29.319
People thought that children were somehow
protected

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:31.509
because they were children

00:03:31.509 --> 00:03:35.249
and in fact, he said, no no, I've seen children who are 
"insane".

00:03:35.249 --> 00:03:39.439
This is interesting because it actually brings a thread
through the work, which we'll come back to,

00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:42.959
which is thinking that, is autism - what is it? Is it a
developmental disorder?

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:45.789
Is it a mental illness, like schizophrenia?

00:03:45.789 --> 00:03:47.239
What is it?

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:49.019
And we'll come back to that.

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:53.949
However the term "childhood psychosis", which
was a more modern term for childhood insanity,

00:03:53.949 --> 00:03:55.690
and childhood schizophrenia,

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.400
became synonymous- anybody who is very very
disturbed but wasn't mentally retarded or

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:00.889
intellectually impaired

00:04:00.889 --> 00:04:04.349
They'd say, "Oh, it's childhood psychosis, it's childhood schizophrenia".

00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:07.859
This continued really in this country until
1980.

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:11.219
Leo Kanner was the first child psychiatrist,
I mentioned, in the country,

00:04:11.219 --> 00:04:13.609
He was actually a refugee from of Europe,

00:04:13.609 --> 00:04:15.620
fled the nazis, was hired at Hopkins

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.100
to bridge the gap between pediatrics and
psychiatry.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.989
He reported his paper in 1943
on eleven children,

00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:26.499
who he said had two things that were were essential.

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:27.909
One was autism,

00:04:27.909 --> 00:04:31.029
living in your own world, being cut off from other
people.

00:04:31.029 --> 00:04:35.580
And he had very interesting, provocative and evocative
ways of talking about this.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.949
On the other side, he said the other thing
that was essential was these were children had

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.180
trouble with change.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.860
How many people actually

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:42.469
ever actually

00:04:42.469 --> 00:04:45.150
seen a child or adult with autism?

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.620
Okay, so, a lot of you.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.189
S,o you know what he's talking about in terms of
change was

00:04:49.189 --> 00:04:53.399
he was saying these were kids who would notice
any little deviation from normal.

00:04:53.399 --> 00:04:58.580
So the parents rearranged the house, the children would get
very upset. Or if they took a different 

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.789
route to school, the child

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:05.740
would get very upset. So we talked about this: literally insisting
on things being the same way.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:11.159
Also however as part of this, kind of the flip-
side in a sense, in terms of an insistence of sameness,

00:05:11.159 --> 00:05:13.960
he said, well another part of its resistance to
change

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.460
he talked about some of the unusual behavior
as we very commonly see

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.120
in younger and more classically autistic
children,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.599
body rocking, hand and finger flicking.

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:25.159
You say well how's that

00:05:25.159 --> 00:05:27.919
resistance to change, how is that insistant on
sameness?

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:30.599
He saw that as an attempt on the child's part

00:05:30.599 --> 00:05:34.289
to maintain the sameness in the world.
You follow that?

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:35.699
So it's a little tricky,

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:38.419
but in fact it's a whole package, this resistance

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:39.979
to change, the insistence on sameness thing.

00:05:39.979 --> 00:05:42.899
It includes many unusual movements and mannerisms.

00:05:42.899 --> 00:05:46.659
Dr. Klin's going to say a little more about that
today, and we're going to talk a lot about it later on

00:05:46.659 --> 00:05:48.049
when we talk some of the research

00:05:48.049 --> 00:05:50.830
that when you think about it, it's very interesting, because he
is saying,

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.479
these are kids who come into the world

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:58.129
and they don't connect with their parents and other people, like
any other baby will starting from birth.

00:05:58.129 --> 00:06:00.959
On the other hand, even though they're not living in the world
of people,

00:06:00.959 --> 00:06:04.279
they're very clued into the world of things,
and we'll have a chance to see a little bit

00:06:04.279 --> 00:06:05.629
about that later today

00:06:05.629 --> 00:06:07.229
with Dr. Klin's talk.

00:06:07.229 --> 00:06:10.930
He thought children were born with autism, he thought it was congenital.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.859
We now know that about twenty percent of the
time,

00:06:13.859 --> 00:06:16.519
children with autism

00:06:16.519 --> 00:06:17.229
seem to be

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:18.069
reasonably okay

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:21.860
and then they seem to either lose skills or they don't
keep developing in the same way.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.189
So it's not so clear that it's totally congenital,
that being said,

00:06:26.189 --> 00:06:30.379
almost always, by the time a child is three years
of age the parents are worried. Ninety percent

00:06:30.379 --> 00:06:33.310
of the time, parents are worried by the time the
child is two.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:35.020
About sixty percent of the time

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.769
parents are worried the child's first year of life.

00:06:36.769 --> 00:06:40.110
And we'll have a whole talk when we'll talk about very young children
with autism.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:43.689
It's a very active area of research around here

00:06:43.689 --> 00:06:47.710
Lastly, if you read his paper, Kanner talked
about the work of a man who's here- literally

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:48.690
here - at Yale.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.389
The building right next door, a man named Arnold Gesell
who's running

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:54.189
what became the Child Study Center back then.

00:06:54.189 --> 00:06:57.259
Gesell was interested in normal baby development.

00:06:57.259 --> 00:07:01.410
Gesell was one of the people charting normal baby development
and he said, you know

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.939
typical babies by six to eight weeks are starting
to smile back to their parents.

00:07:05.939 --> 00:07:09.780
And for those of you who, when you have children you
will discover it's a wonderful occasion

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.729
because for the first time, you've been taking care of this little lump
which just feeds and poops as far you can't tell,

00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:17.409
and all of a sudden, you think, oh my God, there's a person there! 

00:07:17.409 --> 00:07:20.879
And it very characteristically happens around
six weeks of age, and that's true.

00:07:20.879 --> 00:07:23.409
And Kanner talked about that, he said kids with autism-

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:24.629
very different- 

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:27.719
they don't seem to go through that same set of
processes

00:07:27.719 --> 00:07:30.609
And he was remarkably right about that. We'll
talk more about that.

00:07:30.609 --> 00:07:34.149
We now know that actually from the moment of birth, 
the typical baby

00:07:34.149 --> 00:07:37.069
is more interested in the human face and voice

00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:38.749
than anything else in the world.

00:07:38.749 --> 00:07:42.539
I think it's very different in autism.

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:45.329
And here's a quote from Kanner, 1943,
his paper,

00:07:45.329 --> 00:07:48.929
He says, "The outstanding 'pathognomonic,'
fundamental disorder

00:07:48.929 --> 00:07:53.249
is in the children's inability to relate themselves
in ordinary ways to people in situations

00:07:53.249 --> 00:07:54.749
from the beginning of life...

00:07:54.749 --> 00:07:58.580
There's this from the start an extreme autistic
aloneness that, whenever possible,

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:03.080
disregards, ignores, shuts out anything
that comes into the child from the outside."

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.779
And we'll have a chance to talk about that. That's
most true of the youngest children.

00:08:06.779 --> 00:08:10.240
As kids get older things change, and you'll have
a chance to talk about that.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.190
Now some of the things that Kanner talked about
in his first paper...

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:18.809
it's a wonderful paper, but some things misled
people. And these are couple of things

00:08:18.809 --> 00:08:21.859
that exist out there to some extent as urban
myths.

00:08:21.859 --> 00:08:26.779
The first thing was, of the eleven children
ten of the parents

00:08:26.779 --> 00:08:31.199
either were in Who's Who of America or American
Men and Women in Science.

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:34.650
Remarkably successful people.

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:38.990
Right? This led to the notion, especially in the 50's
and 60's that you had a very bright parents

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:40.960
to have a child with autism.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.420
This also led to a somewhat bad place, and some people said, Ah,
it must be because he is so bright, they're not paying

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.900
enough attention to the child.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:50.920
So people blamed the parents for the child's trouble.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.100
In fact, if you think about it

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.110
Kanner was writing in the late nineteenth- he's seeing
cases - in the late 1930's early

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:58.510
1940's.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:03.500
This is how many decades before Al Gore invented the
internet?

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:04.590
Remember that?

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.470
This was the time-

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:08.460
This is an old audience..

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:09.920
Dr. Klin: Exactly..

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.490
Dr. Volkmar: Yeah

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:14.860
I threatened Lisa that I was going to talk about
Trotske and Emma Goldman today,

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:17.910
but she said, oh no, they'll never know about that.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.600
Interestingly, back in the thirties and forties
is no great

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.500
information resource right? Like the Internet is today.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:28.210
How are you going to get to the one person- the
only- child psychiatrist in the country, much less

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:29.290
the one person

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.450
interested in autism?

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.300
You're going to be well connected.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:37.510
They're going to be somebody who's working
in the university or college or are very academic

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:38.440
or in a very successful setting

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:42.940
where you can say to the dean of the medical school
or president of the college or president of

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:45.320
the university, I've got a really unusual child,

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:46.780
is there anybody in the country

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.910
that's studying unusual children?

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:51.030
And the dean or the president would say

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.730
'Geez, I don't know, but I'll ask around.'

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:56.959
And we think that's the reason why there's
this unusual bias

00:09:56.959 --> 00:09:59.070
for getting very successful people.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:03.200
You see children with autism everywhere.
We've now come to appreciate this was a selection

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:04.110
bias.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.620
Children are born into all social classes, I've
seen children from every continent on the

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.250
globe, except for Antarctica,

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:14.360
with autism, I'm sure if there were penguins 
that were social, we'd see autistic penguins.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.000
But you see it everywhere, and it looks remarkably
the same.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.430
Now what differs is how people respond to
it. There's a lot of social, cultural things about

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.540
autism we can talk about terms of treatment.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:28.110
But how it looks is really remarkably the same.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:31.790
Kanner thought autism was not associated with medical
conditions that led people in the fifties and

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:32.579
sixties to say, well

00:10:32.579 --> 00:10:34.730
if you have a medical problem, you can't have autism.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.199
I want to show a slide on this in a little bit.

00:10:37.199 --> 00:10:41.580
Turns out about twenty percent of strictly diagnosed children
with autism develop seizures.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.050
Epilepsy.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.550
Hard to imagine, right?

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:48.090
How can that not be a medical condition?

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:51.780
Well it took people a while because they had to follow
children over time.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:55.050
He thought children with autism- and I'm going to talk about this
this next week-

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:58.930
had normal levels of cognitive ability. Now
why did he think that?

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:03.050
Some things on IQ tests- puzzles- they were
great!

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.459
Now the rest of the test?

00:11:04.459 --> 00:11:07.250
Well, they didn't do so well.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.810
But he said, Well if they did this well on the rest of the test

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:12.590
their IQ would be normal, which is true, but

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.100
-and the 'but' is, they don't.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:16.110
They have unusual peaks

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.420
and valleys in terms of skills.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:22.670
In classical autism, non-verbal skills often
are preserved

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:27.710
where verbally mediated things can be really very
very severely impaired.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.960
I think we'll have a chance to talk about that.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.980
And finally the schizophrenia business.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.960
The word autism had first been used back in
the early 1900's

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:38.679
to talk about some aspects of unusual thinking

00:11:38.679 --> 00:11:40.750
and people's schizophrenia.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.670
So people who thought things that really didn't
make sense; they didn't communicate very well.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:48.060
This confused people into thinking, well, autism
is a kind of schizophrenia.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:53.110
It took people about fifteen / twenty years to
realize that that was not in fact true.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:56.470
And this is a little summary of what happened
over the years.

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:00.770
The most important thing to know is that in the seventies
people in Great Britain started looking at children

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:01.630
with autism

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:05.860
and looking at children with schizophrenia, and said you know,
this is not the same.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:08.780
Kids with autism have trouble from very early in life.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:11.970
Kids with schizophrenia, not only do they look different,

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.550
they hear voices, they look like adults with schizophrenia,

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:17.710
but they don't develop their troubles until
much later.

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:20.690
The classical time to develop schizophrenia is young
adulthood,

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:22.860
Sometimes you see adolescents,

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:27.120
very rarely do you see children. It's much
less common than autism.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:30.410
And when people then looked at family histories
they discovered

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.680
no evidence of increased rates of schizophrenia in 
people with autism,

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:37.410
a lot of schizophrenia in the families of
children with schizophrenia.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.590
So people started making a distinction

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:44.860
by the time something called the diagnostic and statistical
manual which is a very influential

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:46.510
psychiatric guidebook to diagnosis,

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:50.920
the third edition came out in 1980
and they included autism for the first time.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:53.990
Until 1980 you couldn't make a diagnosis
of autism

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:55.410
everybody was thought to have schizophrenia.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.319
It was a horrible mess and muddle

00:12:57.319 --> 00:13:01.390
because we couldn't interpret research, we didn't know who was
being studied.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:05.490
So there's a new category of disorder in DSM-III
it has changed over time,

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.070
they have been through various editions.

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:10.070
This is a very short summary of the current edition
DSM-IV.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:12.720
This came out in in 1994, it's the fourth edition.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.800
You have to have trouble in the social area in terms of
language

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.000
and play and also these unusual behaviors.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.910
It has a good balance of sensitivity and specificity
it works very well,

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:26.180
it works will well around the world.

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:30.240
There was a lot of work that went into DSM-IV,
Dr. Klin, who was then, I think, 

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.940
probably a post-doc,

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:34.450
right?

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:36.700
After that. Even being a post-doc

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:41.530
we actually had a thousand cases, a hundred
raters, twenty-some sites around the world.

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:43.940
It was like Ben Hur and the chariot races.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:48.730
And it continues to be for the moment the
- it's the current definition of

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:49.760
autism that's used

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:53.130
predominately in the United States but also very much around
the world.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.730
They're also - we'll have a chance to talk about this next
week-

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.880
other approaches to definition: rating scales
checklists,

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:01.220
and we will have a chance to talk about some of
these.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:03.960
And again these have their uses, their pros and their
cons,

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.270
One of the problems is, what's the 'gold' standard?

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:09.060
How do you decide somebody really has autism?

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.730
We don't have a blood test,

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:13.180
we don't have any physiological things.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:17.090
Turns out, the so-called 'gold' standard, you should get some 
really experienced clinicians together 

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.600
and and say, Yeah, we think the kid's got autism.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.680
And so it's a bit of a,

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.650
you know, dog chasing its tail in some respects.

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:28.429
We need better and different approaches, and you're
going to hear about some of the work

00:14:28.429 --> 00:14:31.330
a little bit today but more when we talk about
infants,

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:35.740
trying to come up with better screening methods
for finding autism in very young children.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:39.750
I sometimes say, my wife is a pediatrician
it's like somebody came in to her

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:42.230
with an eighteen month old and said, "I'm worried my child is
deaf".

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:46.340
And she pulls out a fifteen item questionnaire.
They'd say, "No, I don't want to fill out some paper and pencil thing,

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.360
I want a hearing test."

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.820
Well we don't have that for autism, but we-
that's one of the things we're aiming for.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:54.689
We would like something, you'll hear a little
bit about it later today,

00:14:54.689 --> 00:14:56.960
a lot more about it if you're in the course later on,

00:14:56.960 --> 00:15:00.500
we're looking for better approaches that are more
robust in terms of screening.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.810
The reason that's important,

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:07.690
for many children, early intervention makes a big, big
difference.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:12.240
And again when we look into it, there's a lot of
other categories that go along with autism,

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.160
these days, this may all change

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:15.820
when DSM-V comes out

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:19.710
We'll have a chance to talk a little bit about
these; I'm going to say just a word about them very

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:20.780
briefly,

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:23.940
Dr. Asperger was working in
1944.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:27.600
He reported a series of boys- he was working, by the way,
in Austria,

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:31.740
so he was totally unaware of the Leo Kanner's work
the year before, here in the States.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:36.290
He wrote about boys who had horrible social troubles-
they couldn't join groups,

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:39.930
but they had good cognitive and language skills.
Unlike children with autism,

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:42.950
whose language is often delayed,

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:47.150
or very unusual or aberrant. They had motor problems; they had very unusual
interests: the kid would know all the trains into

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:48.020
and out of Vienna

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:51.550
The 6:15 stops at Leipzig...

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:55.280
And the kid would have to take his parents to
the train station to be sure the 6:15

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:56.520
got in on time.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:00.830
And he made the point that these interests actually
interfered with a child's learning.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:03.790
And he mentioned there's often a positive family
history

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:07.080
but these were kids whose fathers often were
somewhat socially odd.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:11.500
We'll have a chance to talk about this later
on. I actually think Dr. Klin's going to show some drawings

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:13.379
to give some examples in a few minutes.

00:16:13.379 --> 00:16:18.500
But Asperger's disorders get recognized
as an autism variant- it's in that same category.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:20.910
The difference is, these are kids with good

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:25.850
language. They don't have good communication; they know a
lot of words and are often fixated on a topic,

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:29.930
but they can't necessarily carry on a conversation.

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:32.900
There's a lot of issues in terms of thinking
about Asperger's.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:37.810
Some of the things that Asperger said, the child
talks before he walks, words are his lifeline.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:40.140
He emphasized that it's different in autism, 

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:42.340
although all the social deficits are there,

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:44.760
the language to some extent is also there.

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:49.380
It's interesting, and we'll talk about it in terms
of implications for mechanisms and intervention.

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:51.380
When we looked at it in DSM-IV for this

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:52.290
big study,

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:56.139
in fact we've found some differences in Asperger's
which was the reason it was included,

00:16:56.139 --> 00:16:57.070
at least for the moment.

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.080
There's someone who is going to be on campus in a

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:02.840
few weeks, actually, who's screening the movie 'Adam'

00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:04.269
which just came out, I think, just came out.

00:17:04.269 --> 00:17:05.360
Which is about a young

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:07.340
person with Asperger's-

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:08.670
anyway, it just came out.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:09.790
but there's going to be

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.110
the person who produced the movie is going to be here

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:15.130
on campus. We'll talk about that when it comes
closer.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:19.720
There are a small group of children who
develop normally to three, five, six, seven, eight

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:20.709
years

00:17:20.709 --> 00:17:24.990
and then develop something that looks like
autism. This is very bad news unfortunately.

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:28.000
When you see this- most of these children are
very very poorly-

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:29.810
people used to not believe parents

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:32.510
when they would say, "My child was perfectly normal."

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:34.990
Now with videotape we can go back

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.920
and look- you'll see children who were perfectly fine, and we
may have a chance at some point during the course

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.240
when we talk about this,

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.370
to see some of these video tapes.

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:45.950
The child was perfectly normal and then kaboom- it's like
someone pulls off

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:47.480
the light switch.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:52.230
The child loses skills, often in multiple areas, comes
to look very classically autistic,

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:56.400
and typically, unfortunately, don't get things back.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.480
And finally, Rett's disorder. This was a man
named Andreas Rett who was writing in 1966.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:01.920
These were girls

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:06.970
who had very unusual movements of their hands.
Has anyone here ever seen a patient with Rett's Syndrome?

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:08.350
Okay? Very odd hand movements,

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:11.640
they have breathing problems, they have movement problems,
they have back troubles.

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:16.750
They are normal when they're first born and they
seem to lose skills, they lose their hand movements,

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:20.330
and they become very very very intellectually
impaired

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:22.760
Um,

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:25.520
Rett originally thought this was some kind of autism.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:29.570
In fact, people agreed, it didn't really-
except for the very beginning- look like

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:33.000
autism at all. These were very characteristic
and very different things.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:38.240
We put it in DSM-IV because we thought
was sufficiently interesting and ought to be studied.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:40.560
And subsequent to the time it got put in,

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:45.210
it has been found to be a single gene disorder.
There's a very particular gene called

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:49.460
MECP2, which is a regulator gene which goes ka-phlooey
in Rett's Syndrome.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:54.080
And you can find boys with this, although
it's very unusual, it's probably mostly lethal in boys.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:57.750
And again, it's very interesting as an example
of something that's relatively rare,

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:02.090
but very important because we can understand the
genetic mechanism.

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:03.170
And finally,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:08.400
there's a funny term, PDD-NOS: Pervasive
Developmental Disorder - Not Otherwise Specified.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:13.410
This means kids who have some features of
autism but they don't meet the full definition.

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:16.000
"Close but no cigar autism."

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.320
Now this is a very large group of children-
it's probably on the order of 100 to

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:21.880
150 kids.

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:26.990
But very poorly studied. You can imagine it's hard enough to get
federal money to study autism,

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.390
think of how much harder it will be to get something
that's not quite autism.

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:33.320
There are unlikely to be several sub-types of
this,

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:37.419
but it's a very interesting area of work, and when
you hear those ads on television about how common

00:19:37.419 --> 00:19:38.560
autism is,

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:44.370
they really are talking about the autism spectrum
and especially about PDD-NOS.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:46.809
Let me say a very little bit about epidemiology.

00:19:46.809 --> 00:19:50.950
There's a wonderful chapter, which we'll put
on the web site for anyone who's interested,

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:52.179
by a man named Eric Fombonne,

00:19:52.179 --> 00:19:54.520
from the Handbook of Autism,

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.810
He makes several important points, one
is that various

00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:01.210
- various things happen in terms of looking
at epidemiology.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:04.970
There are important implications of how big your study is,
how small your study is,

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:07.220
Small studies find higher rates

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:09.930
probably because they do a better job of finding
cases.

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:13.470
He also points out that there have been changes in definition
over time,

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:18.230
also there's a problem which is that sometimes parents
want the autism label to get services.

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:20.690
It's a ticket essentially. Remember Willy Wonka

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:24.250
and the Chocolate Factory? It's the golden ticket; 
it gets you a lot of services.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:25.920
And so sometimes parents will push

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:26.679
to get the label

00:20:26.679 --> 00:20:28.530
to get the services.

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:32.570
PDD very broadly defines probably about 1 in 
150 children.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:36.460
Autism is more like 1 in 800 to 1000.
Still not uncommon,

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:40.210
but not as common as the broader PDD group.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:44.169
And this just shows you- this is data
from Frombonne's chapter that I actually graphed out.

00:20:44.169 --> 00:20:48.600
This shows you the rates of autism over time
starting with the first epidemiological study.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.210
But you can see among other things, although things
look like they're going up in terms of the rate,

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:55.029
were also changing the ways we diagnose
autism.

00:20:55.029 --> 00:20:56.840
So we're looking at a moving target.

00:20:56.840 --> 00:21:01.740
And particularly DSM-IV and ICD-10,
which are essentially exactly the same,

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:06.790
we specifically set out to have a system that
worked well for more able, as well as for less

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:07.990
cognitively able people.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:09.880
Which means we're going to pick up kids that

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:12.529
were there, they just weren't picked up before.

00:21:12.529 --> 00:21:16.720
So it's not so clear that the rate has
increased even though it looks like it has.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:21.100
And furthermore you can do something called
confidence intervals around all those same numbers,

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:24.440
and then you see that in fact there's a fair
amount of agreement

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:25.320
overall in terms of

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:29.530
what people are saying in terms of rates.

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:34.210
So there's no question that more cases are being
identified, but the question is, is there a real increase

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:37.900
and there are lots of issues in terms of changing definition,
better diagnosis,

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:43.350
more awareness and its implication for services.
A problem called diagnostics substitution.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.810
I mentioned in neurobiology, early on

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:49.100
people didn't think there was much in terms
of neurobiology, but as they follow children over

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:51.250
time it became clear two things:

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:54.740
one was high rates of seizures disorder,
epilepsy,

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:57.030
pretty clearly a brain-based problem, right? Seizures.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:01.400
The other thing was, as people started looking
at the family members they became aware of a strong

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:03.270
genetic aspect to autism.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:06.200
We're going to have a whole talk on that later in the course.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:07.909
This is data actually from that

00:22:07.909 --> 00:22:09.409
book that I mentioned before.

00:22:09.409 --> 00:22:13.799
This is a sample from here in New Haven of
I think several hundred people with autism.

00:22:13.799 --> 00:22:15.600
This is a sample from Boston.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:20.800
This is data time from a normative British
sample, 5,000 I think,

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:23.740
normal, typically developing children, a normative
sample.

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:27.620
this shows rates of developing seizures by
age, so this 

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:28.390
is first seizures,

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:29.700
rates per thousand,

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:32.440
and here's less than 3,  3 to 6, 7 to 10

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:34.350
obviously once you developed a seizure,

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:35.310
you're going to drop out of this,

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:39.960
it excludes children that have febrile seizures-
if you only have seizures in relation to a fever-

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.610
so, this is really epilepsy.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:45.440
And the normal sample you can see the highest
rate is less than 3 years,

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:46.480
then it goes down, down, down,

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:49.490
by the time you get to be 14,

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:52.029
15, less than 1 in 1000.

00:22:52.029 --> 00:22:56.820
Children with autism either in New Haven or
in Boston, high rates across the board,

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:58.259
including a big group

00:22:58.259 --> 00:22:59.460
early in life

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:02.599
but then another big group in adolescence. It's not at all uncommon
to hear

00:23:02.599 --> 00:23:05.159
a child with autism develop seizures

00:23:05.159 --> 00:23:06.320
as an adolescent.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:10.150
Someone your age who develops seizures, I can tell
you, you'd literally be worked up the wazoo

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:12.360
because it's very uncommon.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.610
Kids with autism, it's actually not at all uncommon.

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:20.150
This was very convincing to people thinking
this was a brain-based disorder.

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:24.130
Medical conditions with autism, there have been a lot
of speculations over the years about people

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:28.610
reporting cases of autism associate with X
syndrome, Y syndrome, Z syndrome.

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:34.240
The strongest associations are with some
disorder like fragile X and tuberous sclerosis,

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:36.410
which are strongly genetic disorders.

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:38.250
And I mentioned, with epilepsy.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:42.560
The problem with many of the early case reports,
is there's a bias in case reports.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:43.590
Autism-

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:45.480
as a journal editor, I get a paper,

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.179
autism associated with down syndrome, 2 papers.

00:23:48.179 --> 00:23:53.260
I might publish that, or I might not. In fact,
I stopped publishing case reports

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.360
for just this reason. But, it's more interesting.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:24:00.860
Somebody sends me a paper on autism not seen
in 2 it down syndrome.

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:01.669
Hey. I'm not

00:24:01.669 --> 00:24:03.260
going to publish that. It's not newsworthy.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:07.299
So it's a problem, there's a bias for people to
be swayed by these positive

00:24:07.299 --> 00:24:08.450
reports of cases

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:09.910
so we have to do more,

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:12.629
larger epidemiologically-based studies,
and when you do,

00:24:12.629 --> 00:24:13.799
you find this.

00:24:13.799 --> 00:24:17.479
So some of the things that people saw early
on- people said, "Oh, there's an autism association

00:24:17.479 --> 00:24:18.930
with Congenital Rubella.

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:23.740
Kids with Congenital Rubella are often
blind or deaf, they're very impaired.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.730
Interestingly, if you look at them over time,
they look less and less autistic

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:30.270
over time.

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:32.460
Lots of interesting neurobiological findings,

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.259
we mentioned the seizure disorder,

00:24:34.259 --> 00:24:37.390
high levels of the blood transmitter called
serotonin

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:40.820
Serotonin is a transmitter in the brain that's also
found in the stomach and in the platelets.

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:43.740
As a group, people with autism have
high levels of serotonin.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:46.669
We'll talk some about that when we talk about
psychopharmacology.

00:24:46.669 --> 00:24:49.430
Some of the medications actually work on
serotonin.

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:53.509
People with autism are more likely to have
unusual reflexes that persist.

00:24:53.509 --> 00:24:56.970
There's some interesting reflexes that you see in babies
that go away.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.810
This one called, for example, a visual routing
reflex

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.149
If I take my pencil 

00:25:01.149 --> 00:25:02.519
or my reflex hammer

00:25:02.519 --> 00:25:04.679
and I make eye contact

00:25:04.679 --> 00:25:07.610
and I go like this, the typical person looks at me like I'm crazy
person.

00:25:07.610 --> 00:25:12.110
A person with autism, not every time and not every
person, but a fair number

00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:13.490
[mouth sucking sound] ... will root.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:16.490
It's like if you take a baby and do the same thing,

00:25:16.490 --> 00:25:19.520
the baby will root. And the idea is that the baby is programmed to look
for the breast.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:21.460
Turns out that the hosts of these

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:23.389
funny little neurological signs

00:25:23.389 --> 00:25:25.780
that you sometimes find in people with autism.

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:29.430
There's some work on changes in the brain,
actually in terms of the brain structure.

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:34.570
Especially around some things called minicolumns
the arrangement of the brain in columns, the cortex,

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:36.330
also something called mirror neurons.

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:39.820
You're going to hear about the fusiform gyrus
and faces in just a second,

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:43.540
and there's a paper that just came out from us,
which has yet to be replicated,

00:25:43.540 --> 00:25:47.160
but it reports high rates of placental abnormalities.

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:51.299
This is a colleague of ours over at OB/GYN who asked
us, Gee, do you have any placentas

00:25:51.299 --> 00:25:53.680
of kids with autism? And they then did a study,

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:58.510
and there are the abnormalities of these unusual
were called trophoblast inclusions,

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:01.790
the persistent kids with autism are more likely to
be there

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:03.080
otherwise they're pretty much gone.

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:07.440
You also see them in kids with genetic
syndromes, things like triple X syndrome or

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:12.120
XXY syndrome, where they've got one to many
chromosomes.

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.820
So lots of areas, and we'll have a chance to talk about
this, we have a whole talk by Kevin Pelphrey

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:17.430
on autism and the brain.

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:20.530
There's a lot of interest in terms of thinking
about brain regions

00:26:20.530 --> 00:26:26.290
and what the implications might be understanding
the social aspects of autism.

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:27.770
And genetics.

00:26:27.770 --> 00:26:32.650
If you look early on there was an impression of no
genetics to autism but

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:36.740
obviously people with autism weren't so commonly reproducing
at that time the outcome was poor. Many people

00:26:36.740 --> 00:26:39.310
with autism ended up in institutional settings.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:42.670
As people started to look, and the first
study was by Rutter and Folstein in the

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:44.200
late seventies.

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:47.690
Identical twins versus fraternal - same sex fraternal twins-

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:51.540
identical twins are always going to be the same sex, right? Because they're
identical-

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:52.240
---

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:53.470
The

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:55.500
fraternal twins could be brother sister.

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:58.990
They exclude those because there's a gender difference
in autism and we haven't talked about 

00:26:58.990 --> 00:26:59.560
but will,

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:03.630
which is that girls, when they get autism
tend to be much more severely affected.

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:06.570
Boys on the other hand are more likely to get autism,

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:09.450
which is something that's interesting thinking about the
neurobiology.

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:10.440
If you look-

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:11.030
and this is all

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:14.920
the twin studies lumped together- again, we're going to have a whole
talk on genetics-

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:17.509
about 60 percent of the time identical
twins will have

00:27:17.509 --> 00:27:20.419
pretty strictly diagnosed autism- if one kid
has autism,

00:27:20.419 --> 00:27:21.940
the other will.

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:24.490
If you start to expand your definition a bit,

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:26.590
you get to 90 percent- not 100 percent-

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:28.450
that's awfully high.

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:31.630
And here are the data on the fraternal twins, same sex.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:34.700
So there clearly is a very strong genetic
aspect.

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:38.929
The complexity as you'll hear, is that probably there
are multiple genes involved

00:27:38.929 --> 00:27:40.919
and people have speculated based on

00:27:40.919 --> 00:27:42.960
mathematical models somewhere between 4 and

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:45.200
20, maybe even more.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:49.660
The interesting thing, as you'll hear when Matt
State comes to talk, is we're actually close to being able

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:51.760
to find some genes out there that have been identified.

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.380
Once we absolutely do have some genes
it'll

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:54.910
be fantastic

00:27:54.910 --> 00:28:01.910
because we can come up with animal models, we can understand
how the genes are expressed in the brain.

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:03.610
Environmental causes in autism.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:09.210
This is an area of tremendous interest,
especially in the lay public and the media.

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:12.400
That's one of those places where there's a lot of smoke
and not so much fire.

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:13.820
Interestingly enough

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:19.460
animal models have been attempted -
it's difficult- most of the animal studies that have been done to date,

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:20.670
involve lesion studies.

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:26.310
Now whatever else is true, it's not as if somebody's
coming along and lesioning the brain of children with autism.

00:28:26.310 --> 00:28:30.640
And so it's a different kind of time course,
it's a developmental thing, and as you'll hear,

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:32.740
when we talk about some of the things with
babies,

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:36.780
there's good reason to think that one of the
things that's so difficult about autism,

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:41.000
is that early lack of social interest then
gets entrained in a lot of other aspects of development.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:43.539
It has a lot of implications for learning.

00:28:43.539 --> 00:28:48.350
Identification of genes clearly will help
because we'll come up with much better models.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:51.860
Immunizations and autism- we're actually having
grand rounds in a couple weeks

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:54.790
with a man who's written a wonderful book called 'False Prophets'

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:57.610
on this whole topic immunizations and autism.

00:28:57.610 --> 00:29:02.520
There's a tremendous amount of evidence that says immunizations
do not cause autism.

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:04.769
Immunizations prevent illness.

00:29:04.769 --> 00:29:06.090
Children who are not immunized

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:07.580
die from

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:09.000
preventable illness

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:12.160
but this- again, a tremendous amount of

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:13.550
misinformation out there.

00:29:13.550 --> 00:29:16.610
People have looked at other things like heavy
metals, mercury,

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:18.390
arsenic, lead,

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:21.460
drugs and other toxins. In fact, the data are
pretty limited.

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:24.409
People also looked at cluster cases.

00:29:24.409 --> 00:29:26.170
You know, Brook township, New Jersey.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:30.940
It's a funny business- I had the experience
a few years ago of having

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:34.840
someone from one of the papers in Hartford call
me and ask me about the outbreak of autism

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:36.710
in Guilford, Connecticut.

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:39.150
And I laughed at the guy- he said, "Why are you laughing?"

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:41.520
And I said, "That's the town I tell anybody who's coming
to Yale,

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:45.410
if they want to live in a good town with
a good school district, you say 'oh, go live in Guilford.'"

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:46.960
So I was actually referring people

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:49.070
with autism to Guilford.

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:50.890
Um, right?

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:55.370
So it's a- it's a funny business. And again on
balance the data for environmental causes

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:57.320
is not horribly strong.

00:29:57.320 --> 00:30:01.010
There's a paper that will show up on the reading list
by Wing and Potter

00:30:01.010 --> 00:30:03.310
that summarizes all this.

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:06.140
Outcome research, very briefly, as time has
gone on,

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:10.520
kids are doing better with early intervention-
we're going to talk about outcome in, I think, about two weeks.

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:14.480
We have more people who are talking, people have
higher levels of intellectual ability,

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:17.600
kids are now going to college. There's a whole set of
books out there now

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:20.790
for kids going to college- I mention the Chapel Haven
program

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:23.159
is for kids who are doing so well, they can

00:30:23.159 --> 00:30:24.830
almost make it independently in college.

00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:26.570
They just need another push

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:29.029
to help them get those life skills up to the point where
they can actually

00:30:29.029 --> 00:30:32.210
go and do it. Often the academic part isn't so challenging

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:34.750
it's the social and the unstructured part that is.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:39.580
There are problems that do remain- it's difficult
in terms of understanding a cure, you'll find a lot of people

00:30:39.580 --> 00:30:41.740
that claim, "oh, my child was cured of autism."

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:45.420
Over the years, seeing thousands upon thousands
of children

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:50.940
probably, I've seen five, that if I met the child
I wouldn't necessarily know that they had autism.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:54.740
But that's 5, out of over seven to 
ten thousand.

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:56.120
So I remember those.

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:57.020
That's not to say people don't do a

00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:58.470
whole lot better,

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:02.580
but often there are some residual problems, which is not surprising-
this is a very serious problem.

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:06.580
But again, and we'll talk about what 'normal' means and what a 'cure' is.

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:07.620
Treatment.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:12.430
There's a very good report- we're going to talk about in
two weeks on treating children with autism

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:14.169
from the National Research Council.

00:31:14.169 --> 00:31:18.769
It looks at all the programs around the country
that have empirical data to show they work.

00:31:18.769 --> 00:31:21.440
There's about ten of these programs, they are wonderful
programs,

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.080
they have some differences, but many things
in common.

00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:28.169
They all focus on structured intensive intervention,
they have some things going for them in terms

00:31:28.169 --> 00:31:29.080
of

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:31.779
working on the kinds of things, early intervention,

00:31:31.779 --> 00:31:33.869
language, communication, joint attention.

00:31:33.869 --> 00:31:35.740
We'll have a chance to talk about that.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:37.559
They do make the point in this report

00:31:37.559 --> 00:31:39.520
that not every child gets better.

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:41.769
Some kids, even despite the programs

00:31:41.769 --> 00:31:44.760
don't do so well and we need to understand
more about that.

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:48.200
But as a group, children are doing better and
better and better.

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:50.900
We'll also have a chance later on, we'll talk about drug treatments.

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:54.120
Drug treatments don't cure autism but they can
help with of some of the symptoms,

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:56.960
and sometimes that actually helps the child
participate in the program

